  "question": {
    "math_academy_id": "q-48801",
    "knowledge_point_id": "0f2818fc-787e-5b46-8a7a-c5fbea7905a6",
    "knowledge_point": "Determining an Angular Rate Given a Related Rate",
    "problem": "Given that $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2$ for $\\theta =\\theta (t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=-3$ at the point where $\\theta =\\pi ,$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$",
    "worked_solution": "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(r) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=-3$ and $\\theta =\\pi ,$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =(-3)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =(-3)\\cdot (-1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
